From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eden PS4 <eden-ps4@localhost>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:04:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4 memory: DRAM from direct memory, sparse tables committed
 on demand, small code caches

What a PS4 app can get (measured by switch-bench): 4.5 GiB of direct memory
(the GPU's pool), well under 1 GiB of flexible memory (behind mmap, all of
it committed when mapped), and executable memory only from the system pool.

- The emulated DRAM (4 GiB) comes from direct memory, in pieces mapped back
  to back into one reserved range.
- SparseLargeVector (the page table: 1 GiB for a 39-bit address space) only
  reserves its range and maps 16 KiB pages as they are written, as on
  Windows. A recompiled read of a page not written yet faults, and a
  signal handler maps a page of zeros there.
- Code caches are 32 MiB per core: they come from the system pool, which
  the app heap shares.
- No fastmem: the PS4 can't map the same memory twice for an app.
